Answer "what can I do here?" in one call — cache-backed and offline after the first run.

This is the first command to run on an unfamiliar project. It is size-tiered,
because a 400 KB capability dump is functionally the same as no capability dump.
data.collection_slugs is NEVER truncated: only the per-collection detail array is
capped, and the truncation warning carries the exact command that pages past it.

SYNOPSIS
  pay explain [--slim|--full] [--collection SLUG]
  pay explain --section connection|collections|globals|commands|query_syntax|exit_codes|gotchas [--offset N] [--limit M] [--grep PATTERN]

DISCOVERED IN THIS PROJECT
  profile=default base_url=- discovery_revision=-
  cache is cold — run 'pay discover' to populate

FLAGS
    --collection COLLECTION      one entity, in full detail
    --full                       every field of every entity; refuses above --max-bytes
    --grep GREP                  case-insensitive substring over the slug and both labels (not a regex)  [grammar plain case-insensitive substring; not a regex]
    -h, --help                   help for explain
    --limit N                    return at most M entries of the selected section (0 = tier default)
    --max-bytes N                size ceiling for --full  [default 409600; range 1024..]
    --offset N                   skip N entries of the selected section
    --section SECTION            connection|collections|globals|commands|query_syntax|exit_codes|gotchas  [values connection|collections|globals|commands|query_syntax|exit_codes|gotchas]
    --slim                       connection + every slug + exit codes only (< 1.5 KB)

  Root flags (every command): run 'pay --help' for the full list.
    --profile --base-url --output --path --quiet --verbose --timeout --deadline
    --no-cache --refresh --yes --dry-run --no-audit --no-redact --errors-to

OUTPUT
  data_kind: capabilities
  .data = {"connection":{…},"collection_slugs":[…all of them…],"collections":[…detail…],"globals":[…],"query_syntax":{…},"exit_codes":[…],"gotchas":[…]}
  Envelope: {"ok":true,"v":1,"command":"…","data_kind":"…","data":…,"meta":{…},"warnings":[]}

EXIT CODES
    0   success
    2   auth — missing, invalid, locked or unverifiable credential
    5   validation / bad input — the request was never worth sending
    6   network — DNS, TLS, timeout or a 5xx from the server
    9   configuration — no base_url, unknown profile, secret in plaintext
    10  capability — this project does not support the operation
  
  Full table: pay explain --section exit_codes

EXAMPLES
  # the whole picture, budgeted for a context window
  pay explain

  # smallest useful answer: connection + every slug
  pay explain --slim

  # page past the 40-entry detail cap
  pay explain --section collections --offset 40

  # one entity in full
  pay explain --collection pages

  # teach yourself the filter syntax
  pay explain --section query_syntax

  # the whole exit-code table
  pay explain --section exit_codes

  # the verified footguns for this Payload version
  pay explain --section gotchas

  # find the CRM collections on a 49-collection project
  pay explain --section collections --grep crm


COMMON MISTAKES
  1. Reading `.collections` and concluding those are all the collections.
     Instead: That array is capped at 40. `.collection_slugs` always holds every slug; page the detail with --section collections --offset N.
  2. Running `--full` on a large project and hitting exit 5.
     Instead: That is the point — it refuses instead of flooding you. Use the default tier, or --collection SLUG for the one you need.
  3. Calling it before every command.
     Instead: It is cached for 10 minutes and served offline; call it once per session, and again after a schema change with --refresh.
  4. Using --grep as a regex.
     Instead: It is a plain substring over the slug and both labels. Use --path or jq for anything richer.

SEE ALSO
  pay collections
  pay describe <collection>
  pay doctor
  pay discover --refresh

